
📘 DFDL Reference – Short Notes

🔹 Purpose

DFDL Reference = IBM ka detailed guide
jo DFDL schema ke rules, objects, annotations, expressions, aur variables samjhata hai.


---

🔹 Key Sections

Concept	Description

Example Schema	Chhota XSD with DFDL annotations — dikhaata hai DFDL structure kaisa hota hai.
Predefined Variables	Built-in variables (e.g., current length, position, encoding) for parsing control.
DFDL Expressions	XPath-like expressions to calculate lengths or conditions dynamically.
DFDL Objects	Building blocks: elements, simple types, complex types, model groups, etc.
DFDL Annotations	Define actual parsing rules (delimiter, length, encoding, justification).
Scoping	Rules ke valid area define karta hai (schema-level, type-level, element-level).
Points of Uncertainty	Parser ambiguity (e.g., multiple choices, similar delimiters) handle karne ke rules.



---

🔹 Example

Input CSV:

101,Pankaj,25000
102,Raj,30000

DFDL Schema Snippet:

<xs:element name="Employee" dfdl:lengthKind="delimited" dfdl:separator="," 
  xmlns:dfdl="http://www.ogf.org/dfdl/dfdl-1.0/">
  <xs:complexType>
    <xs:sequence>
      <xs:element name="Id" type="xs:int"/>
      <xs:element name="Name" type="xs:string"/>
      <xs:element name="Salary" type="xs:int"/>
    </xs:sequence>
  </xs:complexType>
</xs:element>

🟢 dfdl:separator="," → defines comma as delimiter (annotation).


---

🔹 Summary

Normal XSD → only logical structure

DFDL Schema → logical structure + physical format rules (annotations)

Reference Section → explains all DFDL rules, objects & parsing logic.



